The enclosure for the DECpc AXP 150, aka Jensen, was designed and built
by/for Intel. They used it for one of their servers and we just adapted it
to the Alpha. That's why the front panel reset button doesn't do anything,
nobody put that into the design spec. it was an oversight you could say.

Actually, the early Alpha CPU's and regulators generated quite a bit of
heat. The semiconductor group was still working on layout and smart power
stuff for heat reduction. Yes, later chips seemed fine with just a fan, but
there were all kinds of things that cropped up. IN the AlphaServer 2100, the
original model with EISA slots, a wing was used instead of a heatsink for a
memory interconnect controller.

By the by, al Alpha systems, must have the ECU run on them to set the state
of the machine for Windows NT or OpenVMS and Tru64 UNIX. Lot's of people
never do this and then later complain of little glitches or random
happenings or something not quite right, well, I have seen quite a few
faults directly attributable to not having run the ECU, such as, Floppy disk
drive missing, display not working properly, serial port problems,
management port problems, console problems, weird boot problems, it's a long
list.

Speaking of cooling, the AS2100 had a plenum for handling airflow and to
block EMI it used waveguides on the cable egress and ingress.

rtt


