:I obtained a DEC 3000/300...

  I will assume this is a DEC 3000 model 300 series Alpha system.  (There
  are a number of very similarly-named systems, and confusion is common.)

:I noticed that i have some kind of dongle at my
:parallelport, is that any kind of licence, maybe for Tru64? There is also a
:digital logo on it. If oit would be for Tru64, would it then be possible to
:get the cheaper update version of Tru64 (39$) ?

  Neither the hobbyist nor commercial versions of OpenVMS or Tru64 UNIX
  require dongles, I expect you are looking at a terminator of some sort.
  Probably SCSI.  There will very likely be at least one part number on
  the device, please post that value.  (H8574-A is one of the more common
  terminators for the Amphenol 50-based SCSI bus connections, IIRC.  You
  may also find a 12-xxxxx-xx, 29-xxxxx-xx or 57-xxxxx-xx part number.)

..or is it possible to use a crosscable
:and attach it to teh serial port of my x86 linux box?

  The OpenVMS Frequently Asked Question describes the serial console for
  most VAX and most Alpha systems, and includes references to various
  websites and details.

:Next thing i'd try would be NT for Alpha, or Tru64. 

  Microsoft Windows NT for Alpha was retired some years ago, and there 
  are comparatively few Alpha-native versions of applications that were
  ever available -- most any applications must be translated using FX!32.
  Further, Microsoft Windows NT never supported TURBOchannel systems --
  PCI and EISA systems were the supported platforms.

  Tru64 UNIX removed support for TURBOchannel systems circa the V5.1a
  release -- and officially, somewhat earlier than that.  (If you wish
  to use the Tru64 UNIX hobbyist program, you will accordingly be unable
  to load and use a current release on this ancient TURBOchannel box.)

  OpenVMS Alpha maintains support for most (all?) TURBOchannel Alpha
  systems in all current OpenVMS Alpha releases.  V7.3-1 is current.

:The problem is that i am not really willing to pay a lot of money for
:some operating system, as i buyed that computer with a box of beer :-)

