In article <3F37B042.9060104@gwu.edu>, Chris Mattern  <syscjm@gwu.edu> wrote:
> You don't understand what he means by "insert mode".  You have no idea of
> what he is talking about.  You have no understanding of vi whatsoever.
> The opposite of "insert mode" is not "overwrite mode".  The opposite
> of "insert mode" is "command mode".

The distinction between "insert mode" and "command mode" is an illusion.

OK, OK, I'll just spill the beans.

There are three modes in vi, but most people only ever see one of them.

There's "vi mode", "ex mode", and "open mode".

It's very rare that you'll ever leave "vi mode".

In "vi mode" you are always entering a command.

A command consists of an option count, a command letter, and possibly an
argument.

The argument is either text terminated by an escape, text terminated by a
return (for convenience, when a return isn't a possible input), or another
command.

For example, to insert text before the current position, yuo use the insert
command: "iThis is text^[" (where the ^[ is the "escape").

To search for a string, you use the search command: "/Pattern^[". Because
there is only one line in a search command you can use return instead of
escape.

Because the insert command gives immediate feedback rather than staying in the
input line at the bottom of the screen the way video teco did, it feels like
a mode, and is unfortunately described as a mode in much documentation going
back over 20 years... but it isn't really a mode other than (perhaps) the
'incomplete command' mode.

Play with it for a while, try things like "6a123456789^[" or "I<-- ^[...", or
look at what gets undone when you do an "undo" after an insert. If you think
of it as "the insert command I haven't yet finished" and get used to being
in a "command mode" mental mode all the time, you'll find vi a lot friendlier.

"But," you say, "what about arrow keys?"

:map
up      ^[OA    k
down    ^[OB    j
left    ^[OD    h
right   ^[OC    l
:map!
up      ^[OA    ^[ka
down    ^[OB    ^[ja
left    ^[OD    ^[ha
right   ^[OC    ^[la


That's right... they're just macros. They terminate the current command (^[),
move (h, j, k, or l), and start a new insert command (a). They're a
convenience feature, one I find more annoying than useful, not really part
of the syntax.

There's a handful of commands that were added to vi late in the design that
don't follow these rules. I consider two notable:

ZZ	This is just a shortcut for ":x^[".

~	For some reason the switch-case command didn't take a count in the
	old vi. This has been fixed in most current versions. It also doesn't
	take an argument. It seems to have been more a convenience shortcut
	than a true command.

