In article <3F29872F.188CCE5C@devnull.com>,
Chris Quayle  <lightwork@aerosys.co.uk> wrote:
> I think maybe you are looking at this from the wrong end. If you get
> yourself into the state of mind where you always write in strict ansi C,
> without exception, then your code should compile under any compiler that
> claims to be ansi compatible.

Why, I must admit that you have articulated a truth so fundamental that
it must almost be considered an axiom. And, yet, I'm afraid that you are
still far from enlightenment.

For it is the social aspect of GCC that we are concerned about here. That
is to say the question to be addressed is not "what do I need to do when
I am writing code", but "what is the result of a large number of developers
writing code for GCC". For, as you must realise, the majority of such
developers will not be so scrupulous in their habits, and will write code
that works for them with nary a concern for the next fellow who might
find himself in need of the software in question.

Which brings one inevitably to the conclusion that it is contrary to the
best interests of companies like HP or Sun to make it hard for hobbyists,
and others who have the most time to spare in the creation or conversion
of open source software, to use their best compilers. For it does seem to
be in their interests that their customers and potential customers are
assured of an ample supply of those utilities and applications, obscure
and otherwise, that flow from the keyboards of those whose avocation is
the creation of such things.

And I must say that after one has spent a few hours reading Brust one finds
that Paarfi's elegant and convoluted manner of writing has become, if not
second natutre, at least to seem almost natural.

