This book picks up where the computer science classic "The UNIX 
Philosophy" leaves off.  Every chapter has been updated for the Linux 
market. New chapters have been added on Windows, Open Source, and other 
interesting developments.  This is not a "how-to" book on Linux.  There
are plenty of those out there.  No, this book explains the "whys" of Linux:

	* Why Linux power users prefer the command line over point-and-click
	* Why Linux system designers still adhere to the small tools philosophy 
of Unix
	* Why Linux uses flat text files for everything
	* Why Linux's portability gives it a far bigger edge than you'd imagine


