quite), might be in a position to address that issue, as clearly it
causes confusion. 

I did (until today) think the licence was free on SPARC if you bought
the system directly from Sun or a Sun reseller, or the system was x86
based, due to this wording:

"Now you can use the Solaris 9 Operating System at home or at work --
without paying a license fee. For only the cost of media plus shipping
or download related costs, you can use the software for non-commercial
usage on single processor systems supplied to you by Sun or its
authorized distributors or based on the x86 architecture."

at 

http://wwws.sun.com/software/solaris/binaries/index.html

Someone at Sun has stated on this thread Solaris 9 is legal for home
use on SPARC. 


You have yet another interpretation, which means it is not free for
*long-term* home use even on x86. 

I now think you are right, since those words I quoted above are under
the section "Developers and Educational Users - Get started with the
Free Solaris[sm] Binary License Program today!"

After I read the actual licence conditions at:
http://wwws.sun.com/software/solaris/binaries/bcl.html
I think you are right, and one needs to be a developer or educational
user to use Solaris 9 for free. Using their definition of educational
user, at the above location, I conclude that a home user teaching
himself Solaris, is not an educational user. If you develop code under
Solaris, (i.e. you are a developer) you can only use the license for
the development. So I'd conclude a developer can't use his developer
license to write a letter to his mother. 

So my view of this 'so-called' free license has become even lower.
Perhaps I'm wrong. Even Sun's employees don't seem to know what the
conditions are, so what hope do we have ?

