> When people are producing better code using gcc than vendor compilers,
> its because 1) they don't know the options for the vendor compiler too
> well, or 2) they have a program whose execution time is dominated by
> one or two loops and gcc happens to handle the particular way that code
> is written better or 3) they aren't using some of the higher end
optimization
> features like interprocedural optimization, profile based optimization,
> etc.  Often just reording the C code or changing arrays to pointers or
> vice versa, etc. can change the performance significantly.  Certain code
> constructs just fit what the compiler expects to see for given
optimizations
> better than others.  Those who experiment reap the benefits.

Agree.

IBM's xlc gives better peak-performance on AIX.
I expiremented with mpg123, bzip2 and gzip for getting a thumb.
IBM xlc broke the binaries of mpg123 and bzip2 with:
"-O5 -qarch=(cpu) -qtune=(cpu) -qipa" but worked with
"-O3 -qarch=(cpu) -qtune=(cpu)" and lost against the GCC
with "-O6 -mcpu=(cpu)"
After isolating the broken module and optimizing the remaining at highest
level
IBM xlc won in all CPU's against GCC. Took me two or three hours for this
really small count of modules.
Don't wan't this with OpenSSL with it's hundreds of objects.

>
> Whether the numbers you see at SPEC are realistic is another matter,
> most people (and many commercial apps) just use -O or -O<big number>
> rather than checking what options are out there.  gcc tends to be
> relatively better in these limited circumstances (mainly because it
> rolls a lot more stuff in the -O and -O2 than vendor compilers for
> code quality/safety reasons)  Profile based optimization is a pain,
> one of my major pet peeves with SPEC is that it allows this for the
> 'base' numbers.
>

Agree.

And the compiler vendors give the routines used in the SPEC special
attention that they don't break the code even at the highest optimization.

On AIX the difference between "-qarch=com" and "-qarch=604 -qtune=604" gives
often more than 20% on a PPC604.
How can one be sure that such peak optimized code works correct in all
circumstances, since it cannot be debugged or tested in full. At highest
level nearly all modules give a warning of the optimizer.
No problem if mpg123 crashes one day with a special mp3, but most apps are a
little more critical.

Don't think the time for a full optimization of the "Golden Code" is really
a problem, but for what CPU?
It should be possible to relink the app on the target with 90%-95% common
code and the 5-10% optimized for the target by the choice of only one
archive, but never seen such with any commercial software.

And when I build opensource like OpenSSH, OpenSSL the makefiles lack any
good optimization for AIX.
It is nearly inpossible to separate the one or two modules that broke perl
or OpenSSL after a possible warning in each module and a successful linking.
One big pound of the GCC is it's ability to generate optimized code still
containing the debug info that you can see what the optimizer has broken,
without stepping down to the assembler code.
So the overall answer for the best compiler is: "it depends on..."
And 15 days out of 60 days with IBM xlc are over now.

One fact is: People pay a lot money for 10% faster cpu(s)...
No matter if home user or SAP user.

