You are making the mistake that a large number of people make -
including one Sun employee on this thread. 

Solaris 9 for x86 can be downloaded for $20. One can obtain a free
binary licence for Solaris 9 x86, so the total cost of legally using
Solaris 9 x86 is $20 - even for commercial use. You only pay one $20
for downloading, no matter how many times you install it.

With SPARC however the situation is VERY different. You can download
Solaris 9 for SPARC for no charge, BUT (and it's a very very big BUT
indeed), the free binary licence for Solaris 9 (SPARC) is only
available subject to two conditions that mean it is effectively
useless for any home user. 

The two conditions that prevent this "free" licence being of any
practical use are to home users are:

1) The machine must have been supplied to you either directly from Sun
or from a Sun authorised distributor.

2) The machine must be capable of having only 1 CPU put into it. 

This is a very big shift from the Solaris 8 license, which allowed for
the use of Solaris 8 for commercial or non-commercial use on any SPARC
machine, as long as it did not have more than 8 CPUs. 

I can legally use Solaris 9 on a PC made by HP (one of Sun's
competitors in the UNIX market) for minimal cost. I can't however use
Solaris 9 on a workstation on which Sun have previously made a profit!
Does that seem illogical, or does that seem illogical?


